  "id": "DrugBank_DB13874 - MONDO_17893",
  "verbalization": "Enasidenib is a drug that has been associated with the treatment of Acute Myeloblastic Leukemia (AML) through multiple pathways. The evidence suggests that Enasidenib may be repurposed for Inherited Acute Myeloid Leukemia (IAML) due to its potential to target the underlying molecular mechanisms driving AML. Specifically, Enasidenib is indicated for AML with maturation, a subset of AML characterized by a specific chromosomal translocation, and it is also indicated for AML with the T(8;21)(q22;q22) translocation, which is a well-known driver of IAML. This indicates that Enasidenib may have a role in treating IAML by targeting the molecular pathways involved in AML progression.\n\nThe evidence from the visible paths highlights that Enasidenib interacts with Mitoxantrone, a drug commonly used in the treatment of AML, and that both drugs are associated with IAML. This suggests a potential mechanism of action involving the inhibition of certain pathways that are shared between AML and IAML. The presence of overlapping indications and the shared molecular targets imply that Enasidenib may be a viable candidate for repurposing in the treatment of IAML, particularly in patients with the T(8;21)(q22;q22) translocation.\n\nThe integration of these pathways supports the hypothesis that Enasidenib may exert its therapeutic effect through mechanisms that are relevant to IAML, although the exact molecular mechanism remains to be fully elucidated. The evidence is consistent with a mechanistic link between Enasidenib and IAML, but further studies are needed to confirm the precise role of Enasidenib in this context and to establish its efficacy in treating IAML.",
